Where an adapter sleeve mounts a bearing onto a plain shaft, a withdrawal sleeve is used where the shaft is stepped or has a defined bearing seat. The sleeve is driven into the gap between the shaft and the taper bore of the bearing to seat it.

Its real value shows at removal. Because the sleeve can be drawn back out under controlled force, the bearing comes off without the hammering and heat that so often damages both bearing and shaft. For maintenance-heavy installations that is frequently the deciding factor in choosing a withdrawal sleeve over an adapter sleeve.

We stock withdrawal sleeves across common shaft sizes and supply non-standard sizes for replacement and retrofit work.
